WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.370
Good APIs prevent misuse before it starts.

2
00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:04.425
Instead of passing raw indexes, raw
lengths,

3
00:00:04.505 --> 00:00:06.220
and unrelated buffers around loosely,

4
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:08.742
Rust code can use types that represent
validated input,

5
00:00:08.822 --> 00:00:11.523
slices that carry length, iterators that
control traversal,

6
00:00:11.603 --> 00:00:13.318
and result types that express failure.

7
00:00:13.398 --> 00:00:16.360
Validated input types are especially
useful at parser boundaries.

8
00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:19.453
Once a raw message, path, identifier, or
size field has been checked,

9
00:00:19.533 --> 00:00:23.274
converting it into a domain type prevents
every downstream caller from repeating the

10
00:00:23.354 --> 00:00:24.134
same validation.

11
00:00:24.214 --> 00:00:27.747
The type communicates that the value has
crossed a trust boundary under specific

12
00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:28.138
rules.

13
00:00:28.218 --> 00:00:30.401
Slices and iterators can carry bounds
naturally.

14
00:00:30.481 --> 00:00:33.858
A function that accepts a slice receives
both a view of the data and its length.

15
00:00:33.938 --> 00:00:37.575
A function that accepts an iterator can
process values without knowing or trusting

16
00:00:37.655 --> 00:00:38.798
manual index arithmetic.

17
00:00:38.878 --> 00:00:42.359
These shapes often reduce the need for
callers to coordinate separate buffers,

18
00:00:42.439 --> 00:00:43.530
lengths, and positions.

19
00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.208
Encapsulation protects internal buffers
and invariants.

20
00:00:46.288 --> 00:00:49.510
A type can own its buffer and expose
operations that maintain valid state.

21
00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.136
Callers do not need to know the internal
capacity, offset,

22
00:00:52.216 --> 00:00:53.619
or partially parsed structure.

23
00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:57.024
They interact with safe methods that
either succeed or return clear errors.

24
00:00:57.104 --> 00:01:00.845
Owned results can be safer than
caller-managed output buffers when the ownership and

25
00:01:00.925 --> 00:01:03.315
size relationship would otherwise be hard
to express.

26
00:01:03.395 --> 00:01:07.136
Caller-managed buffers may be appropriate
for performance-sensitive boundaries,

27
00:01:07.216 --> 00:01:08.723
but they require clear contracts.

28
00:01:08.803 --> 00:01:12.180
Defensive API design makes the safe path
natural and the risky path explicit.
